      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "To find the last three digits of \\(3^{12000}\\), we need to determine \\(3^{12000} \\mod 1000\\). We are given that \\(3^{400} \\equiv 1 \\pmod{1000}\\). This means that the exponent 12000 can be expressed as \\(12000 = 3 \\cdot 400 + 200\\). Therefore, we can write:\n\n\\[3^{12000} = (3^{400})^3 \\cdot 3^{200}\\]\n\nSince \\(3^{400} \\equiv 1 \\pmod{1000}\\), we have:\n\n\\[(3^{400})^3 \\equiv 1^3 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{1000}\\]\n\nSo, we only need to find \\(3^{200} \\mod 1000\\). To do this, we can use Euler's theorem, which states that \\(a^{\\phi(n)} \\equiv 1 \\pmod{n}\\) for \\(a\\) and \\(n\\) coprime. Here, \\(\\phi(1000) = 1000 \\left(1 - \\frac{1}{2}\\right) \\left(1 - \\frac{1}{5}\\right) = 400\\), so \\(3^{400} \\equiv 1 \\pmod{1000}\\). This implies:\n\n\\[3^{200} = (3^{400})^{0.5} \\equiv 1^{0.5} \\equiv 1 \\pmod{1000}\\]\n\nThus, \\(3^{200} \\equiv 1 \\pmod{1000}\\), and we have:\n\n\\[3^{12000} \\equiv 1 \\cdot 1 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{1000}\\]\n\nTherefore, the last three digits of \\(3^{12000}\\) are \\(\\boxed{001}\\).",
